= Bayesian inference =

== Overview ==
'''Bayesian inference''' is a framework for updating uncertainty about hypotheses, parameters, or models by combining prior information with a likelihood model to obtain a posterior distribution.

== Bayes' rule ==
For hypothesis H and evidence E:

<pre>
P(H | E)
= P(E | H) P(H) / P(E)
</pre>

where:

* P(H) is the prior probability;
* P(E|H) is the likelihood term;
* P(H|E) is the posterior probability.

== Prior ==
The prior represents uncertainty before the current evidence is incorporated.

Priors can be based on previous data, scientific knowledge, hierarchical structure, regularization goals, symmetry, or subjective judgment.

Therefore:

<pre>
prior ≠ arbitrary guess by definition
</pre>

== Likelihood ==
The likelihood describes how compatible observed data are with parameter/model values under the assumed data-generating model.

It is not the posterior probability of those values.

== Posterior ==
The posterior combines prior and likelihood information.

It can be used to estimate parameters, make predictions, compare models, or support decisions.

== Prediction ==
Bayesian prediction averages over posterior uncertainty:

<pre>
P(new data | observed data)
= ∫ P(new data | θ) P(θ | observed data) dθ
</pre>

== Bayes' theorem and Bayesian inference ==
Bayes' theorem is a probability identity.

Bayesian inference is a larger modeling framework requiring choices about model structure, likelihood, prior, computation, checking, and interpretation.

== Limitations ==
Bayesian results depend on the adequacy of the model and prior assumptions.

A precise posterior from a poor model is not automatically reliable.

